  "query_preview": "..Seeing a very abnormally moisture rich airmass\nacross Utah and southwest Wyoming this evening due to the remnants\nof Tropical Storm Rachel, which have moved north of the area and\nare starting to phase with an elongated low currently over the\nPacific Northwest. That system's associated cold front has nosed\ninto northwest Utah, where some additional precipitation has\nstarted develop. Across the remainder of the forecast area,\nearlier widespread rain, heavy at times, has become much more\nscattered in nature and more focused over the higher terrain. \n\nAt least some showers from today's round of precipitation will\npersist overnight, but overall there should be a relative lull for\nmuch of the state during the overnight hours. The exception will\nbe northwestern portions of the area seeing increasing coverage\nfrom the front. This round of precipitation will become more\nwidespread during the day Monday as the low continues to move into\nthe area, bringing increasing instability as it interacts with\nRachel's moisture. \n\nThis low is not forecast to open up and thus is expected to \nslowly wobble across Utah and southwest Wyoming through Tuesday, \nbringing continued widespread precipitation, particularly for \nnorthern portions of the forecast area, as snow levels continue to\nlower, dropping to 8,000 feet or perhaps lower later Monday into \nTuesday. Snow accumulations could be significant, but primarily \nconfined to the highest terrain. Otherwise, this trough will be \ndropping high water totals in areas that have been already \nsaturated by the first round. Thus, the threat of flooding will \nremain elevated, with the Flood Watch remaining in effect across\nthe vast majority of the area.\n\nBy early Wednesday, the center of the low is finally expected to\nmove out of the forecast area. However, it will continue to be\nmoving slowly so anticipate some showers to linger, particularly\nfor eastern portions of the area. The cold temperatures associated\nwith this low are expected to bring minimum temperatures down to\nor below freezing across some outlying valleys, including the\nCache, possibly as early as Tuesday morning, with the potential\nincreasing Wednesday and Thursday mornings as cloud cover\ndecreases. \n\nDrier air is finally expected to make its way back in beginning\nThursday as broad ridging develops off the Pacific coat. This will\nbring a steady drying trend along with gradual warming. Maxes,\nexpected to remain well below normal for much of the week, are\ncurrently expected to be near seasonal normals for the upcoming\nweekend.",
